  "slug": "archaea-duplication-d927",
  "summary": "Proteogenomic and comparative genomics studies are recovering coding content and flagging annotation artifacts directly from sequence evidence.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is building coding inventories from sequence evidence rather than relying on per-genome reference annotation alone. A proteogenomic study of Chlamydomonas nivalis integrates transcriptome data with sORFs from a related-species genome to discover small encoded peptides in the absence of a reference genome. A comparative study maps verified protein complex components to orthogroups across 31 eukaryotes, identifying 184 universal orthogroups and releasing the PCOC suite. A bacterial proteome analysis applies MMseqs2 clustering to define core protein sets and treats length outliers as likely annotation artifacts, including those arising from frameshifts. What is new is the use of cross-species genomic evidence, orthology, and clustering to distinguish genuine coding content from annotation error. These works differ in organism, data type, and objective, so the shared element is evidence-based inventory rather than a single common method.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-11"
}
